Abstract Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) and cystic fibrosis (CF) are both slowly progressive cholestatic liver diseases characterized by fibro-obliterative inflammation of the biliary tract. We hypothesized that dysfunction of the CF gene product, cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR), may explain why a subset of patients with inflammatory bowel disease develop PSC. We prospectively evaluated CFTR genotype and phenotype in patients with PSC $(n{=}19)$ compared with patients with inflammatory bowel disease and no liver disease $\scriptstyle(n=18)$ , primary biliary cirrhosis $(n{=}17)$ , CF $(n{=}81)$ , and healthy controls $\scriptstyle(n=51)$ ). Genetic analysis of the CFTR gene in PSC patients compared with disease controls (primary biliary cirrhosis and inflammatory bowel disease) demonstrated a significantly increased number of mutations/variants in the PSC group $37\%$ vs $8.6\%$ of disease controls, $\scriptstyle P=0.02$ ). None of the PSC patients carried two mutations/variants. Of PSC patients, $89\%$ carried the 1540G-variant-containing genotypes (resulting in decreased functional CFTR) compared with $57\%$ of disease controls $(P{=}0.03)$ ). Only one of 19 PSC patients had neither a CFTR mutation nor the 1540G variant. CFTR chloride channel function assessed by nasal potential difference testing demonstrated a reduced median isoproterenol response of $14\mathrm{mV}$ in PSC patients compared with $19\mathrm{mV}$ in disease controls $\scriptstyle(P=0.04)$ and $21\mathrm{mV}$ in healthy controls $\scriptstyle P=0.003,$ ). These data indicate that there is an increased prevalence of CFTR abnormalities in PSC as demonstrated by molecular and functional analyses and that these abnormalities may contribute to the development of PSC in a subset of patients with inflammatory bowel disease.
## Introduction
Cystic fibrosis (CF) is a progressive autosomal recessive disorder caused by defects in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, which encodes for a cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP)-regulated chloride channel (Welsh et al. 2001). Mutations in the CFTR gene cause loss or impairment of CFTR-mediated ion transport across epithelial cell membranes. Since the cloning of the CFTR gene in 1989, more than 1000 gene mutations have been reported (Cystic Fibrosis Mutation Data Base: http://www.genet.sickkids.on.ca/cftr/).
CF affects many organs including the respiratory tract, pancreas, sweat glands, intestine, liver, and the male reproductive tract. Although less frequent than pancreatic and pulmonary disease, liver involvement among CF patients ranges from $9\%$ to $37\%$ , and after pulmonary disease, is the second leading cause of death in adult CF patients (Colombo et al. 1994, 1998; Kerem et al. 1990; Nagel et al. 1989; O’Brien et al. 1992). Electrophysiologic, immunohistochemical, and genetic studies have established that CFTR expression in the biliary tract is restricted to the apical membrane of the bile duct epithelial cells or cholangiocytes (Cohn et al. 1993; Grubman et al. 1995). CFTR dysfunction causes decreased chloride secretion into the bile canaliculi, which, in turn, results in hyperconcentration and acidification of bile leading to obstruction of intrahepatic bile ductules by inspissated secretions and subsequent cholestasis (Grubman et al. 1997). In some CF patients, this is followed by focal inflammation and scarring of the bile ducts and, eventually, focal or multilobular biliary cirrhosis.
Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is a slowly progressive cholestatic liver disease characterized by fibroobliterative inflammation of the biliary tract and leading to cirrhosis and portal hypertension and is a major indication for liver transplantation (Lee and Kaplan 1995). Although the etiology and pathogenesis of PSC is not known, $75\%-$ $80\%$ of cases are associated with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD; Broome et al. 1996; Farrant et al. 1991). Conversely, $2.5\%-7.5\%$ of patients with IBD develop PSC (Lee and Kaplan 1995).
PSC and CF liver disease have several features in common. Both conditions affect intrahepatic bile ducts by inspissated bile secretions, chronic low grade inflammation, and portal tract damage. This results in segmental dilation of intra- and extra-hepatic bile ducts with subsequent stricturing, beading, and eventually biliary cirrhosis. Indeed, a large majority of patients with CF liver disease have cholangiographic features similar to those found in PSC (Colombo et al. 1998; Durieu et al. 1999; Nagel et al. 1989; O’Brien et al. 1992). McGill et al. (1996) attempted to determine whether there was an association between these two diseases and reported a trend toward increased CFTR gene mutations $(10.6\%)$ in PSC patients, but without statistical significance. However, they genotyped for only 32 of the most common mutations out of over 1000 known CFTR mutations.
Although CF is generally considered a multiorgan disease, recent genetic advances have demonstrated that there may be atypical forms of CF with milder phenotypic expression or monosymptomatic (confined to one organ or system) conditions resembling the CF phenotype. Atypical CF or CF-related conditions are frequently associated with rare and milder CFTR mutations and result in isolated male infertility, pancreatitis, or chronic sinusitis (Durie 1998; Welsh et al. 2001; Zielenski 2000). Since CFTR is expressed in the bile duct epithelium and plays a key role in the development of cholestatic liver disease in some CF patients, we hypothesized that abnormalities in the CFTR gene may predispose a subset of IBD patients to develop PSC. In this study, we have evaluated whether PSC is associated with CFTR dysfunction based on genotype and phenotype assessments of CFTR compared with a disease control group consisting of patients with another cholestatic liver disease (primary biliary cirrhosis; PBC) and IBD patients without evidence of liver disease, classic CF patients, and healthy controls.
