## Patient evaluation
Institutional review board approval was obtained at both hospitals. Evaluations of all eligible subjects included a history and physical examination and CF phenotype testing with measurement of sweat chloride concentration and nasal transepithelial potential difference (NTPD) testing. Patients with PSC, PBC, IBD, and CF underwent CFTR genotype analysis.
## CFTR mutation/variant screening (genotyping)
Total human genomic DNA was isolated from peripheral blood cells by standard methods (Gilbert and Vance 1998). Blood samples were coded, and genotyping was performed in a blinded fashion. Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)-based multiplex heteroduplex (mHET) gel shift analysis on MDE gel matrix (BMA, Rockland, Me., USA) was utilized for the detection of CFTR mutations (Zielenski et al. 2002). The analysis included all CFTR exons, their flanking intron sequences, and the promoter region ${\bf\Pi}_{\sim1\mathrm{kb}}$ upstream of exon 1). Fragments displaying aberrant migration patterns were further characterized by direct sequencing analysis with the Thermo Sequenase Radiolabeled Terminator Cycle Sequencing Kit (Amersham-Life Science, Cleveland, Ohio, USA). This screening protocol detects approximately $95\%$ of mutations (Zielenski et al. 2002). Three variants (9T, 7T, and 5T) of the polythymidine tract (T-tract) in intron 8 and the biallelic variant 1540A/G (M470 V) in exon 10 were also tested. The 5T variant in intron 8 correlates with significantly increased exon 9 skipping (i.e., producing incomplete CFTR mRNA transcripts missing exon 9) compared with the 7T and 9T alleles (Chu et al. 1993). Cuppens et al. (1998) have described potential functional differences for the two CFTR variants $470\mathbf{M}$ and $470\mathrm{V}$ (A or G at nucleotide position 1540, respectively), with the $470\mathrm{V}$ variant resulting in decreased functional CFTR.
## Materials and methods
Patient selection
Nineteen patients with PSC, 17 with PBC, and 18 with IBD without evidence of liver disease were enrolled at the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, Mass. Subjects between the ages of 18 and 80 were invited to participate. Eighty-one CF patients and 51 healthy controls were recruited prospectively at The Hospital for Sick Children, Toronto, Canada. All CF patients had classic CF based on the diagnostic criteria established by the CF Foundation (Rosenstein and Cutting 1998). The criteria for the diagnosis of PSC included the presence of typical cholangiographic abnormalities of PSC involving intra-hepatic bile ducts or the common bile duct either segmentally or extensively (Farrant et al. 1991; Porayko et al. 1991). Patients were excluded if there were biliary cal
## Nasal transepithelial potential difference
Nasal transepithelial potential difference measurements were performed by using the technique described by Knowles et al. (Knowles et al. 1981). A baseline maximum potential difference was obtained under the inferior turbinate to reflect the activity of inwardly directed sodium transport at the apical surface of respiratory epithelial cells. An inhibitor of sodium reuptake, amiloride, was perfused to depolarize the membrane and assess sodium transport. Subsequently, a chloride-free solution was used to measure basal chloride secretion, which, in normal epithelial cells, creates a more negative potential difference. Isoproterenol, a stimulator of cyclic-AMP mediated chloride secretion through CFTR, was then added to chloride-free media. The change in potential difference following chloride-free media plus isoproterenol $(\Delta C l+\mathrm{Iso})$ ) directly represents the assessment of CFTR chloride secretion. Genotyping data was not known at the time of nasal potential difference testing.
Sweat chloride
Measurement of sweat chloride concentration by the quantitative pilocarpine iontophoresis method of Gibson and Cooke (Gibson and Cooke 1959) was conducted at approved North American CF centers. Based on recommendations from a consensus statement by the United States Cystic Fibrosis Foundation, a sweat chloride concentration greater than $60\mathrm{mmol}/1$ is classified as abnormal and between $40{-}60\mathrm{mmol/l}$ is borderline (Rosenstein and Cutting 1998). All subjects underwent two measurements of sweat chloride concentration, and the two values were averaged.
Statistical analysis
Genetic test results were summarized as number and percent of patients and were compared between groups by using Fisher’s exact tests; $95\%$ exact binomial confidence intervals (CI) are reported. Observed genotype numbers versus the expected Weinberg-Hardy equilibrium distribution were compared by using chi-square tests. Phenotype measurements were summarized as median and interquartile range (IQR: 25th and 75th percentiles) and were compared between groups by Kruskal-Wallis and Wilcoxon rank sum tests. All $P$ -values were two-sided. The analysis used SAS version 8.0 (SAS Institute, Cary, N.C.).
## Results
Demographics
Nineteen patients with PSC, 17 with PBC, and 18 with IBD and no evidence of liver disease were enrolled in this study. The reference group consisted of 81 patients with CF and 51 healthy controls. The demographics for the patients with PSC, PBC, and IBD are detailed in Table 1. For patients with PSC, the mean age at time of enrollment was 45.6 years; $74\%$ of patients were male. Consistent with previous reports (Broome et al. 1996; Farrant et al. 1991), $68\%$ of the patients with PSC had underlying ulcerative colitis,
$16\%$ had Crohn’s disease, and $16\%$ had neither. Of the patients with PSC, $89\%$ had both intra- and extra-hepatic ductal involvement. PBC patients were primarily female $(88\%)$ and Caucasian $(88\%)$ , whereas patients with IBD were equally male and female and primarily Caucasian $(94\%)$ .
With regards to the sample size of this study, under the hypothesis that disease controls will have a CFTR gene mutation/variant rate similar to the $5\%$ carrier rate and that about $40\%$ of PSC patients will have a CFTR mutation similar to that observed in other adult onset diseases association with CFTR, such as chronic sinusitis (Wang et al. 2000) and idiopathic pancreatitis (Cohn et al. 1998), a study with 24 subjects per group would provide $82\%$ power to detect this eight-fold increase in CFTR mutations by using a Fisher’s exact test with a two-sided $\scriptstyle\mathtt{\alpha=0.05}$ . Though our enrollment of 19 PSC and 35 disease control subjects was slightly off this target of 24 subjects per group, the study size was reasonable for the magnitude of the effect that was hypothesized to be detected.
Genetic testing
Seven of 19 $37\%$ ; $95\%$ CI, $16\%-62\%)$ PSC patients had a single CFTR gene mutation or variant. This is in contrast to three of 35 $9\%$ ; $95\%$ CI, $2\%-23\%)$ ) disease controls (3 IBD, 0 PBC) where two IBD patients had a single mutation or variant and one was homozygous for $-729\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\to}\mathrm{G}$ . (PSC vs disease controls: $\scriptstyle P=0.02$ ; Table 2). All seven PSC patients with CFTR mutations/variants had underlying IBD. This included a subject with CF features of recurrent sinusitis and nasal polyps. Of the three IBD patients with mutations/variants, one with a single allelic mutation had recurrent sinusitis and nasal polyps, whereas the other two had no features suggestive of CF.
Four of the seven mutations/variants detected in PSC patients $(3849+10\mathrm{kb}$ $\mathrm{C}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{T}$ , Highsmith et al. 1994; $2572{\cdot}26\mathrm{A}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{G}$ , Tzetis et al. 1997; I1139 V, Vankeerberghen et al. 1998; IVS8-5T, Chillon et al. 1995), are known disease-causing mutations associated with a range of CF or CF-like phenotypes. S686Y and I1366F are novel potentially disease-causing variants that have not been reported from screening thousands of CF and healthy patients (http://www.genet.sickkids.on.ca/cftr/). The R75Q variant is currently not listed as a CF-causing mutation, although its contribution to CF-like phenotypes cannot be excluded. Two other variants were found upstream of the CFTR gene (–741T/G and –729C/G) in IBD patients used as disease controls. One of them (–741T/G) has been reported to be located in a putative AP-1-binding site. The variant sequence was shown to have an abnormal binding pattern with AP-1 transcription factor or AP-1-related proteins and could affect CFTR expression through altered interaction with undefined trans-acting factors (Bienvenu et al. 1995).
Analysis of the $1540\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{G}$ variant revealed a significant difference in its genotype distribution between PSC and disease controls $scriptstyle{\mathcal{P}}=0.03$ ; Table 2). Interestingly, there is an increase of the 1540G variant-containing genotypes (AG and GG) that produce the $470\mathrm{V}$ CFTR protein variant (less functional). Specifically, $89\%$ $95\%$ CI, $67\text{\textperthousand}$ $99\%$ ) of PSC patients carried either AG or GG, whereas only $57\%$ $95\%$ CI, $39\%-74\%)$ ) of disease controls had these two genotypes $(P{=}0.03)$ . Only one of 19 PSC patients had neither a CFTR mutation/variant nor the $470\mathrm{V}$ containing genotype. In both PSC patients and disease controls, the observed genotype numbers were not significantly different from the expected Weinberg-Hardy equilibrium, further supporting the significance of the increase of G-containing genotypes (AG, GG) in PSC patients compared with disease controls.
