The sweat chloride concentration (given as: median in $\mathrm{mmol}/1$ [interquartile range]) was 21 [14,28] in PSC patients, 24 [15, 32] in the combined disease control group, 28 [21,35] in PBC patients, 22 [11,25] in IBD patients, 16 [10,26] in healthy controls, and 89 [67,105] in patients with CF (Fig. 2). No statistically significant differences were seen between PSC and healthy and disease controls. One PSC patient and three disease controls (two with PBC and one with IBD) had borderline abnormal results (i.e. $40{-}60\mathrm{mmol/l}_{,}$ ). Sweat testing was not completed in one PSC and three disease control subjects because of poor compliance.
## Discussion
In this prospective study, we have demonstrated an increased prevalence of CFTR functional and genotype abnormalities in PSC subjects compared with disease controls. The impairment in chloride secretory response observed in PSC patients was intermediate to that seen in CF patients and healthy control subjects. A similarly reduced chloride response has been reported in other single-organ disorders associated with CFTR dysfunction, such as congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (male infertility; Chillon et al. 1995; Dumur et al. 1996), idiopathic pancreatitis (Cohn et al. 1998; Sharer et al. 1998) and chronic rhinosinusitis (Wang et al. 2000). Nasal potential difference testing is more sensitive than sweat testing for the assessment of chloride ion secretion in disease states characterized by single-organ involvement associated with mild CFTR mutations. Hence, it is not surprising that, except for one PSC patient with a borderline sweat chloride test, all others had normal sweat chloride values.
CFTR mutations have been grouped into five classes according to their effects on CFTR function (Tsui 1992). Classes I–III are considered to be severe mutations and result in complete loss of chloride channel function through various molecular mechanisms, whereas mutations belonging to Classes IV and $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ result in milder CF phenotypes (Tsui 1992). The mutations observed in PSC patients were mild (belonging to Classes IV and V) and affected only one allele. Four of the seven PSC patients with mutations/variants had known disease-causing mutations $(3849+10\mathrm{kb}$ $\mathrm{C}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{T},$ , Highsmith et al. 1994;
$2572{\mathrm{-}}26\mathrm{A\mathrm{\rightarrow}G}$ , Tzetis et al. 1997; I1139 V, Vankeerberghen et al. 1998; IVS8–5T, Chillon et al. 1995) associated with a range of CF or CF-like phenotypes. The remaining three PSC patients had potentially disease-causing mutations (S686Y, I1366F, R75Q), which may contribute to CF-like phenotypes. Indeed, these three PSC patients had lowered chloride secretory responses (3,14, $14\mathrm{mV}$ ) suggesting that these genotype abnormalities resulted in functional consequences. Moreover, the median chloride secretory response in the seven PSC patients with mutations/variants was overall lower than in the PSC patients with no mutations. Of PSC patients, $89\%$ had the $470\mathrm{V}$ (1540G) variant, which is less functional than the $470\mathbf{M}$ (1540A) CFTR variant and may lead to an overall net loss of CFTR function (Cuppens et al. 1998). Only one of $19\mathrm{PSC}$ patients had neither a CFTR mutation/variant nor the $470\mathrm{V}.$ -containing genotype. It is important to point out that what may be regarded as a variant, i.e. not proven to be a disease-causing mutation leading to the classic form of CF, may be a causative mutation leading to the expression of disease in other milder CF related disorders, such as CBAVD and pancreatitis. For example, S686Y, which results in a change of serine to tyrosine, is a potentially disease-causing mutation because it would abolish the consensus phosphorylation site for both PKA and PKC protein kinases. Hence, we feel that the high incidence of mild CFTR mutations and variants detected in our PSC patients may contribute to the pathogenesis, especially since this is associated with statistically significant functional abnormalities in CFTR.
Girodon et al. (Girodon et al. 2002) recently reported that only four of 29 $(13.8\%)$ PSC patients were heterozygous for CFTR disease-proven mutations compared with eight of 115 healthy controls $7\%$ ; $P{=}0.4)$ . Only mutations established as causing classic CF were reported. However, if mild mutations/variants were included, nine of 29 $(31\%)$ of PSC patients had CFTR genotype abnormalities, a finding similar to that of our study. The other major difference between Girodon’s study and ours is that no functional analyses of CFTR were conducted by Girodon et al. (Girodon et al. 2002). In addition, the diagnosis of PSC in some patients was based on liver biopsy results and not on cholangiography, the gold standard, and therefore some patients may have had other forms of cholestatic liver disease. Moreover, one third of the PSC patients did not have underlying IBD.
It is interesting to note, in the current study, that CFTR variants were detected in three IBD controls and none of the PBC control patients. Since the onset of PSC in patients with IBD is variable, it is possible that some of the IBD patients will develop this condition in the future. This may be especially true for the IBD patients who were found to have CFTR variants and who are all relatively young (24, 32, and 41 years; Table 2). These patients will be followed closely for clinical or biochemical evidence of liver abnormalities.
The finding that the median chloride secretory response was significantly lower in the PSC patients compared with healthy and disease controls and in PSC patients with mutations/variants compared with PSC patients with no mutations/variants indicates that there is a functional defect in CFTR associated with a single allelic mutation. Other research groups have shown that single allelic mutations result in impaired CFTR function and are associated with a statistically significant increase in resistance to choleratoxin-induced intestinal fluid secretion in CF-knockout mice (Gabriel et al. 1994), with an increase in sweat chloride concentration in human infants (Farrell and Koscik 1996), and with the development of CF-related diseases in humans, such as asthma (Davis 1984) and chronic pancreatitis (Cohn et al. 1998; Sharer et al. 1998). The decrease in pancreatic-juice bicarbonate/chloride secretion and water output in infants heterozygous for $\Delta\mathsf{F}508$ mutations suggests that a similar impairment in the biliary tract may occur in the setting of a single allelic mutation (Massie et al. 2000).
The pathogenesis of PSC is not known; however, its strong association with IBD ( $84\%$ in this cohort) suggests that chronic portal bacteremia might lead to chronic biliary tract infection, inflammation, and portal and bile duct fibrosis (Lee and Kaplan 1995). If this were the sole mechanism, then all patients with IBD should develop PSC. However only $3\%-7\%$ of IBD patients have or will develop PSC. This may be related to the finding that, in addition to its effect on ductular fluid secretion, CFTR dysfunction results in an excessive host inflammatory response attributable to increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines and neutrophils (Balough et al. 1995; Khan et al. 1995; Muhlebach et al. 1999). This explains the presence of chronic inflammation in the lung, even in the absence of infection (Khan et al. 1995). In an analogous manner, inspissated biliary secretions in combination with excessive biliary tract inflammation secondary to CFTR dysfunction could predispose patients with IBD to develop PSC. Our data suggest that CFTR may play a role as a modifier in the development of PSC. However, this may not be the only mechanism and does not exclude other independent or coexistent environmental or genetic factors or disorders of immune regulation (Lee and Kaplan 1995).
